From b70489528fec654254b0bc439cfebdc8fe35d8d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 07:09:44 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Patched editions: interpreter on, own memory stick, overlays
 compared again

Three gaps found playing a disc mod in a packaged copy, on Windows and on a
Linux handheld, and switches to look for more.

- begin() turns the interpreter on for the start. The units a patch changes
  leave the recompiled code and run under it, so where a release build or a
  packaged layout turns it off (PSPRECOMP_NO_INTERPRETER) the first call
  into a changed unit stopped the game.
- The patched disc's memory stick is <memory stick>-patched beside the one
  in use, also when <PREFIX>_MEMORY_STICK_DIR named it. A packaged copy
  names its own, so its mod saves went to the release's memory stick.
- A patched overlay is compared with the release's image again on every
  cache flush (recompare_patched_overlays), as the executable is. A patch
  may write hooks into an overlay once it runs; the comparison at install
  missed them and those words ran as the release's code.
- <PREFIX>_TRACE_PATCHED=2 logs every changed word, with the nearest
  function a port registered at or below it (a port's replacement of a
  function the patch changes runs instead of the patch).
- Diagnoses: <PREFIX>_PATCHED_INTERPRET_ALL (1, main or overlays) runs all
  of that code under the interpreter, as an emulator would;
  <PREFIX>_PATCHED_INTERPRET_RANGE=lo-hi narrows the executable's part;
  <PREFIX>_PATCHED_COMPARE_VBLANK=1 compares the code at every vblank too.

Checked with Dissidia 012 and a disc mod on Windows: a release build in a
packaged layout starts the mod, saves in the -patched memory stick, and
finds about 70 more changed words in the battle and menu overlays. The
release disc plays as before. The overlay comparison was not run on Linux.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
